3.1. Development and Testing.  Subject to your compliance with this License, Kepler grants you a non-exclusive, non-transferable, non-sublicensable, revocable right to use, build, and modify the Enterprise Features for your own Development and Testing, and to submit patches to Kepler for inclusion in the upstream Mastra software.
3.2. Production Use.  Production Use of an Enterprise Feature is permitted only if, and only for so long as, both of the following are true:
(a) you (and any entity you represent) have entered into, and remain in compliance with, a written agreement with Kepler that expressly permits that use of that Enterprise Feature, including any additional restrictions set out in that agreement. A written agreement that permits use only for stated limited purposes — for example internal enablement, training, evaluation, demonstration, proof-of-concept, or other not-for-resale use — satisfies this Section 3.2(a) to the extent of, and only to the extent of, the purposes it states; and
(b) a valid License Key issued by Kepler enables that Enterprise Feature for you, and your use is within the scope, permitted purpose, and expiry recorded on that License Key.
Production Use of an Enterprise Feature in the absence of either condition is unlicensed and is a material breach of this License.

3.3. Modifications.  You may modify the Enterprise Features for your own Development and Testing. You agree that Kepler and its licensors, as applicable, retain all right, title, and interest in and to all such modifications. Nothing in this Section grants you the right to make Production Use of a modification, which remains governed by Section 3.2.
3.4. Reservation of Rights.  You are granted no rights other than those expressly stated in this License. Kepler and its licensors retain all right, title, and interest in and to the Enterprise Features, including all intellectual property rights in them.
3.5. Restrictions.  Except as this License or Your Agreement expressly permits, you must not:
(a) copy, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, sell, rent, lease, or otherwise make available any Enterprise Feature to any third party;
(b) make an Enterprise Feature available to third parties as a hosted or managed service;
