(d) circumvent, disable, or interfere with any License Key, entitlement check, or other technical measure by which Kepler enables or verifies an Enterprise Feature, or use an Enterprise Feature without a License Key where a License Key is required.

4. LICENSE KEYS.
4.1. Issuance.  License Keys are obtained from Kepler. Kepler issues you one License Key, which enumerates each Enterprise Feature enabled for you together with the scope, permitted purpose, and expiry of that enablement. Enabling an additional Enterprise Feature, or ceasing to enable one, is a change to your existing License Key; it does not require a new License Key and does not create a separate license document.
4.2. Purpose Limitation.  A License Key may be issued for a limited purpose, including evaluation, development, demonstration, not-for-resale, or design-partner use. Where a License Key records a permitted purpose, use of the enabled Enterprise Features outside that purpose is not licensed.
4.3. Expiry.  A License Key expires on the date it records. On expiry, the Enterprise Features it enabled cease to be licensed for Production Use unless and until a valid License Key is issued.
4.4. Non-Transferability.  A License Key is issued to you alone. You must not share, publish, transfer, or sublicense a License Key, or permit any third party to use one, and you must treat it as confidential.
4.5. Verification.  Kepler performs License Key checks during the build and deployment phase. Kepler does not represent or warrant that License Key validation will not be performed at runtime, and Kepler may change the timing and method of License Key validation on a prospective basis. Kepler may issue, amend, limit, suspend, or revoke a License Key so that it enables only the Enterprise Features you are then entitled to use, including on expiry of a subscription term, on a downgrade, on termination of Your Agreement, or on non-payment that remains uncured after any notice and cure period in Your Agreement.
4.6. Entitlement.  Your entitlement to an Enterprise Feature is determined by Your Agreement, not by the contents of a License Key. Accordingly:
(a) if a License Key enables an Enterprise Feature for which no executed written agreement is in effect, that Enterprise Feature is not licensed for Production Use, and you must cease Production Use of it and notify Kepler promptly on becoming aware; and
(b) if an executed written agreement covers an Enterprise Feature that your License Key does not yet enable, you are entitled to that Enterprise Feature, and Kepler will update your License Key to reflect it without undue delay.
4.7. Continued Use After De-Provisioning.  Where Kepler disables or revokes a License Key, or an Enterprise Feature is otherwise disabled or de-provisioned, you will cease all use of the affected Enterprise Feature whether or not License Key validation is technically enforced at the time of use. Continued use is unlicensed, is a material breach of this License, and entitles Kepler to the remedies in Section 7 in addition to any remedy under Your Agreement, including payment of Kepler’s then-current list fees for the affected Enterprise Feature for the period of that use. Continued use does not create a license by course of dealing, and Kepler’s forbearance in enforcing this Section is not a waiver.

5. COMPOSITION OF THE ENTERPRISE FEATURES.
5.1. Designation.  Kepler determines which components, modules, features, and functionality are designated as Enterprise Features, and may add to, remove from, or otherwise recompose that set from time to time.
5.2. Prospective Effect.  A change under Section 5.1 takes effect prospectively, and will not reduce the scope of features made available under an order form in effect on the date of the revision through the end of the then-current license term. During such a term, an Enterprise Feature that you are then entitled to continues to be made available to you in accordance with Your Agreement, notwithstanding that Kepler has ceased to designate it as an Enterprise Feature or has recomposed it. Nothing in this Section restricts Kepler from modifying, improving or altering an Enterprise Feature.

